WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.009
Film Courage: Let’s say I finish my first
draft…what am I doing?

00:00:04.009 --> 00:00:05.910
Who am I showing it to?

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:06.910
What am I thinking?

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:10.040
Vicki Peterson, co-author: Well the very first
thing you should do when you finish a first

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.500
draft is go out and celebrate, truly.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.770
There are so many people who start a draft
and never finish.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:23.660
And by just finishing a draft you’ve probably
put yourself in the top ten percent of all

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.720
screenwriters just by doing that one thing.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.780
And seeing something to completion, seeing
a first draft come to life that’s a really

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.570
huge accomplishment, especially for a first-time
writer.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.670
So we really encourage people to take note
of that, to celebrate.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:46.450
And then I would say after that take a little
break from writing so that you can be fresh

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.890
so that you can come back to it with new eyes.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:56.809
Now the first draft is just the very, very,
very beginning of a very long journey towards

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:00.879
being produced or being a professional screenwriter.

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:04.709
And so after you’ve had a chance to take
a break it’s really important to go back

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:11.319
to that draft and look at it through the eyes
of an editor to really look at it objectively

00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:13.189
so that you can rewrite it.

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:14.189
And in our book [Notes to Screenwriters: Advancing
Your Story, Screenplay, and Career With Whatever

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:17.630
Hollywood Throws at You] we have a whole chapter
on rewriting and things that you can do to

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.670
get your script ready.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:25.829
When it’s ready to be seen by eyes other
than your own we generally recommend that

00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:29.999
this is where you would send it to your writer’s
group, this is where you would get professional

00:01:29.999 --> 00:01:35.999
feedback where you really need mentorship,
where you really need somebody who can guide

00:01:35.999 --> 00:01:38.639
you through the rewrite process.

00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:46.040
And once that happens, once you have a working
draft that you can send out, that’s when

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.840
you start contacting producers, that’s when
you can start sending it out to festivals

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.890
and contests and things like that.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:02:02.489
Generally speaking we find that people have
more success working with producers (independent

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:06.759
producers) and teaming up with people in that
way as opposed to trying to get an agent or

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.340
a manager right out of the gate.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:13.510
It’s pretty rare that that scenario happens
with a first-time writer.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:19.760
So work with the connections that you have,
make sure that you’re networking, make sure

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.650
that you’re sending it to people that you
know.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:28.280
And of it really is truly ready to send out
to the world, you should be getting back some

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.269
pretty positive responses at that time.

00:02:30.269 --> 00:02:36.430
Barbara Nicolosi, co-author: I would say also
that the thing about a first project is you’re

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.719
not writing it to be made.

00:02:38.719 --> 00:02:41.909
You’re writing it to be your calling card.

00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:47.890
So every writer needs to have a couple of
scripts that they can show as this is an example

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.060
of what I can do.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:55.090
I know my craft, I know the business of this
art form, I can martial people’s emotions,

00:02:55.090 --> 00:03:01.370
I can take a character on a journey because
nobody is going to buy your first script or

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:02.799
your first two scripts.

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:04.079
You’re looking to get work.

00:03:04.079 --> 00:03:05.900
You’re looking for people to say “Wow.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:06.900
You know what you’re doing.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.980
I have this little project and we need a writer
for it.”

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:22.219
So that’s why you don’t want your first
project to be your kind of weird, highly-stylized

00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:25.890
passion project because nobody wants to make
that.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:30.209
They’re looking to see can you do a good
basic job at your craft.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:31.799
We need a writer to deliver.

00:03:31.799 --> 00:03:37.239
Vicki Peterson: That’s strong in tone and
genre and can really deliver strong characters

00:03:37.239 --> 00:03:38.459
and a plot that moves.

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:42.519
Barbara Nicolosi: So a lot people have the
wrong sample script, they have the wrong idea

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:45.920
of it and then they are just constantly holding
out hope that somebody is going to buy this

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.209
script and they miss the fact that no, that’s
not what it’s for.

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:52.180
For somebody starting out, you’re trying
to get work and you’re trying to show you

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:56.159
know your business, you know your field.

00:03:56.159 --> 00:03:57.489
We stress that with writers all the time.

00:03:57.489 --> 00:04:03.230
The truth is the first script probably nobody
should ever see, your second script very rarely

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:04.319
should anybody see.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:11.049
But okay now you’re in your third project,
your fourth project and now you’re getting

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:13.370
stuff that should be seen.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:16.519
But again, know what you’re doing with it.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:21.389
This is a sample of your best work, it’s
a sample of what you can do.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:28.860
So again, two different projects, one is more
your particular voice the other one really

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:37.110
shows us as Vicki says you understand genre,
you understand the basic structure of storytelling

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:42.370
because that’s this mysterious thing to
producers, directors and actors and they don’t

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:45.580
know where that comes from and they need writers
who can do that.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:50.389
And so you’ve got to be able to show I’m
one of those people who can do that.

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:54.060
The main point of creating this is to be your
sample.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.120
Vicki Peterson: And what’s also extremely
important is that this is your first script

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.330
and you’ve gotten it to a place where it
is ready to show people.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:05.520
Typically the best thing you can do at that
point is to set it aside and write something

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:06.669
else.

00:05:06.669 --> 00:05:10.400
And write your second script and get that
to a place where you can show people because

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:17.080
then you have two things that you can show
because this business is funny that way.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.960
They see something that they like that is
maybe not for them, they’re going to say

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.750
“Wow, I like this writer.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:23.750
What else do they have?”

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:29.039
And if you don’t have anything else then
that says that you’re not ready yet to be

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:30.039
a professional.

00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:31.880
So you really need to have some things under
your belt.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:35.430
Barbara Nicolosi: And oh by the way your first
project probably should be a feature.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.220
Your first project should probably be a short
film.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:44.259
Or an Internet web series that are five 2-minute
episodes.

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:49.410
Or something that kind of moves you into the
feature realm.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:54.890
The feature storytelling is the hardest kind
of visual storytelling to do so why would

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:56.599
you start with that.

00:05:56.599 --> 00:05:59.879
You know, you’ve got to walk before you
can run.

00:05:59.879 --> 00:06:03.750
But you’ll have more confidence as you have
these little projects and then you work your

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.580
way up and say “Now I’m ready to write
my feature.”

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.880
It’s amazing how many people start writing
an epic scope…

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.780
Vicki Peterson: LAWRENCE OF ARABIA!

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.389
Barbara Nicolosi: A hundred million dollar
big-budget thing and it’s their first project.

00:06:15.389 --> 00:06:17.509
And it’s like you’re just not ready to
do that.

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:19.920
Again, go back to the metaphor of the architect.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.610
Don’t design a skyscraper as your first
thing and your first project needs to be small

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:26.800
in scope so you can get better.

